     "type": "intro",
     "title": "The Glue of the Universe",
     "content": "Right now, every atom in your body is held together by an invisible force so strong it makes gravity look like a rounding error. It is the same force that makes a balloon cling to your hair, that shocks you on a dry doorknob, and that flashes across the sky as lightning.\n\nThat force is **electricity** — and it all begins with a single property of matter called **electric charge**.\n\nIn this lesson you will learn what charge actually is, why it comes in two flavors we call **positive** and **negative**, and the two simple rules that govern it: **like charges repel, opposite charges attract**.\n\nThen you will meet **Coulomb's law**, the elegant equation that predicts exactly how hard two charges push or pull. You will see why two everyday-sized charges could push with billions of newtons, why atoms hold together, and why — despite all this power — gravity still ends up running the cosmos.\n\nLet's start with the thing itself: charge.",
