     "type": "concept",
     "title": "What Is Electric Charge?",
     "content": "**Electric charge** is a fundamental property of matter — as basic as mass. You cannot break it down into anything simpler; it just is one of the built-in features of the particles that make up the world.\n\nCharge comes in two kinds. We call them **positive (+)** and **negative (−)**. These names are just labels — Benjamin Franklin picked them in the 1700s — but the two-sided nature is real and deep.\n\nInside every atom:\n\n• **Protons** carry positive charge and sit in the dense central **nucleus**.\n• **Electrons** carry an equal-sized negative charge and swarm around the outside.\n• **Neutrons** carry no charge at all — they are neutral.\n\nThe proton's charge and the electron's charge are exactly equal in size but opposite in sign. That precise balance matters enormously: an atom with equal numbers of protons and electrons is **electrically neutral**, its charges perfectly cancelling out.\n\nWe measure charge in a unit called the **coulomb (C)**, named after the physicist you will meet later. One coulomb is actually a huge amount of charge — the charge on a single electron is a tiny sliver of it.\n\nSo when we talk about \"charging\" something, we almost never create charge. We simply move some of these charged particles — usually electrons — from one place to another.",
